  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the retrieval:\n\n**FastAPI request validation chain** (`github.com/sg-evals/fastapi--0.109.0`):\n- `fastapi/dependencies/utils.py` \u2014 core: `solve_dependencies`, `request_body_to_args`, `request_params_to_args`, `get_body_field` (nested-model embedding, default-value fallback via `deepcopy(field.default)` on missing keys)\n- `fastapi/routing.py` \u2014 `get_request_handler`, Content-Type negotiation (`content_type_value = request.headers.get(\"content-type\")`), `await request.json()` body deserialization\n- `fastapi/_compat.py` \u2014 Pydantic v1/v2 compat shim; `ModelField.validate`/`.serialize` via `TypeAdapter`, union/scalar-field detection helpers\n- `fastapi/encoders.py` \u2014 `jsonable_encoder` (model\u2192dict, `exclude_none` semantics)\n- `fastapi/params.py` \u2014 `Body`, `Query`, `Form`, `File` field-info classes (embed, media_type, defaults)\n- `fastapi/exceptions.py`, `fastapi/exception_handlers.py`, `fastapi/utils.py` \u2014 `RequestValidationError`, 422 response shape\n- `fastapi/dependencies/models.py` \u2014 `Dependant` model\n\n**httpx request encoding chain** (`github.com/sg-evals/httpx--0.26.0`):\n- `httpx/_content.py` \u2014 `encode_request`/`encode_json`/`encode_urlencoded_data`/`encode_multipart_data` (uses stdlib `json.dumps`, no Pydantic-aware None handling)\n- `httpx/_models.py` \u2014 `Request.__init__`/`_prepare` (Content-Type/Content-Length header assembly)\n- `httpx/_client.py` \u2014 public `request`/`post` etc. surfacing `json=`/`data=`/`content=` params\n- `httpx/_types.py` \u2014 `RequestData`/`RequestContent` type aliases (plain `Mapping[str, Any]`, no schema awareness)\n- `httpx/_multipart.py`, `httpx/_api.py` \u2014 multipart boundary handling, module-level request helpers\n\nThe key drift point I found while reading: `jsonable_encoder`'s `exclude_none` default is `False` (FastAPI keeps `None` keys on output), while a caller building an httpx `json=` payload from `model.model_dump(exclude_none=True)` or a hand-built dict simply omitting optional keys will hit FastAPI's nested-model field-presence checks in `request_body_to_args` differently \u2014 `value is None` explicitly triggers the default-fallback branch, but a *missing key* on a nested embedded body triggers `AttributeError`/`get_missing_field_error` instead, since `received_body.get(field.alias)` returns `None` either way at the top level but nested Pydantic sub-model validation (via `_type_adapter.validate_python`) distinguishes \"key absent\" vs \"key present with null\" for `Optional[X] = None` vs `Optional[X]` with no default under discriminated unions.",
